{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act causes for criminal activity, theft and human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or held civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, illegal drug activity or human trafficking on the premises, after a three-day notice to quit; suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies; covers the tenant and any person the tenant knowingly permits to reside on the premises, continuously or occasionally.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order, for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, or rule or lease violations, with three months' notice for at-will or year-to-year tenancies and one month for month-to-month.","key_value":"3 months' notice (at-will or year-to-year); 1 month (month-to-month); 3 days for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating causes","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, including eviction where the landlord shows the tenant is holding over after the lease and gives written notice of delivery of possession.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)). There is a further requirement that the owner give at least three (3) working days’ notice to the County Welfare Board before starting the eviction action for any resident  (N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for owner-occupancy and demolition evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months; a landlord who evicts to board up, demolish or permanently retire a building must not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year bar on residential use; up to $10,000 per violation","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who give notice of intent to personally occupy a dwelling unit or to permanently board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation of the five-year restriction; the property may not be registered as a planned real estate development during the five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the  landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months. If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal prohibition (civil rights of tenants)","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by evicting, substantially altering a lease or its terms, or refusing to renew a lease when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains to a government authority about health or safety violations, or joins a tenant organization.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes; owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings are excepted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all dwelling rentals except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).  The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ninety-day notice to quit before eviction after foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; before evicting, a landlord must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit when a buyer who will personally occupy the property purchases it, and a new owner must notify tenants of the change within 10 business days.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice of change of ownership","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirement.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or service-disabled veterans, and who lived in a converting building at least one year before the conversion was recorded, may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is below the stated threshold.","key_value":"40 years' protection; family income not more than 3 times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled tenants, or veterans 60 percent or more disabled from service, resident at least a year before the recording.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":"Tenants in Hudson County may be eligible for additional protected tenancy under the Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59).","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"Extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; Hudson County is currently the only qualified county.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County conversion buildings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
